'''Port Waikato''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] which existed for four parliamentary terms from 1996 to 2008, and was recreated by the 2019/20 electoral redistribution ahead of the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 election]]. It was held by [[Bill Birch]] for one term, and by [[Paul Hutchison (politician)|Paul Hutchison]] for the following three terms. From 2020, it was held by [[Andrew Bayly]]. All of these were members of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].

During the campaign for the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]] candidate died. Only a poll for the party vote was held during this election; the electorate vote was determined in the [[2023 Port Waikato by-election]].

==Population centres==
The {{NZ election link|1996}} was notable for the significant change of electorate boundaries, based on the provisions of the Electoral Act 1993.<ref>{{cite act |title=Electoral Act 1993 |type=Act |number=87 |date=17 August 1993 |url=http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/whole.html#DLM307519 |access-date=30 June 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150710153411/http://www.legislation.co.nz/act/public/1993/0087/latest/whole.html#DLM307519 |archive-date=10 July 2015 |df=dmy-all }}</ref> Because of the introduction of the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) electoral system, the number of electorates had to be reduced, leading to significant changes. More than half of the electorates contested in 1996 were newly constituted, and most of the remainder had seen significant boundary changes. In total, 73 electorates were abolished, 29 electorates were newly created (including Port Waikato), and 10 electorates were recreated, giving a net loss of 34 electorates.

The Port Waikato electorate was formed from parts of the {{NZ electorate link|Franklin}}, {{NZ electorate link|Raglan}}, and {{NZ electorate link|Waikato}} electorates, all of which were abolished.<ref name="Electorate Profile">{{cite web |title=Electorate Profile Port Waikato |url=http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/088E7D5A-A724-4C7E-8EA3-8CEBDEF46C15/176/Port_Waikato1.pdf |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliamentary Library]] |access-date=8 July 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929102421/http://www.parliament.nz/NR/rdonlyres/088E7D5A-A724-4C7E-8EA3-8CEBDEF46C15/176/Port_Waikato1.pdf |archive-date=29 September 2007 |page=3 |format=PDF |date=October 2005}}</ref> In its initial area, towns with more than one polling booth were [[Huntly, New Zealand|Huntly]], [[Pukekohe]], [[Ngāruawāhia]], [[Tuakau]]<!-- Harrisville School appears to belong to Tuakau -->, and [[Waiuku]]. Localities with a single polling booth were [[Aka Aka]], [[Awhitu]], [[Bombay, New Zealand|Bombay]], [[Buckland, New Zealand|Buckland]], [[Glen Massey]], [[Glen Murray, New Zealand|Glen Murray]], [[Glenbrook, New Zealand|Glenbrook]], [[Horotiu]], [[Mauku, New Zealand|Mauku]], [[Meremere]], [[Naike]], [[Ohinewai]], [[Onewhero]], [[Orini]], [[Otaua, Waikato|Otaua]], [[Paerata]], [[Pōkeno]], [[Port Waikato]], [[Pukekawa]], [[Pukemiro]], [[Pukeoware]], [[Puni, New Zealand|Puni]], [[Raglan, New Zealand|Raglan]]<!-- including Te Mata School, which is 9km south of Raglan (not to be confused with the locality on Coromandel Peninsula) -->, [[Rangiriri]], [[Ruawaro]], [[Taupiri]], [[Te Ākau]], [[Te Hoe]], [[Te Kauwhata]], [[Te Kohanga]], [[Te Kowhai]]<!-- surprising - that appears to be located in the heart of the Hamilton West electorate -->, [[Te Pahu]], [[Te Uku]], [[Waerenga]], [[Waikaretu]], [[Waikokowai]], [[Waingaro]], [[Waipipi]], [[Waiterimu]], [[Waitetuna]], [[Whatawhata]], and [[Whitikahu]].<ref name="1996 election" />

==History==
[[Bill Birch]] was the first representative of the Port Waikato electorate following the 1996 election. Throughout his long parliamentary career, which started in {{NZ election link year|1972}}, Birch always represented the electorate in which the town of [[Pukekohe]] was located, where he had established a business prior to entering parliament.<ref>{{cite press release |last=Birch |first=Bill |date=8 October 1999 |title=House: Valedictory of Rt. Hon. Sir William Birch |url=http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/HL9910/S00039/house-valedictory-of-rt-hon-sir-william-birch.htm |location=Wellington |agency=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |access-date=13 June 2015}}</ref> When Birch retired at the {{NZ election link|1999}}, he was succeeded by [[Paul Hutchison (politician)|Paul Hutchison]], a medical specialist. When the Port Waikato electorate was abolished in 2008, Hutchison transferred to the reconstituted {{NZ electorate link|Hunua}} electorate, which he represented until his retirement from politics at the {{NZ election link|2014}}.<ref>{{cite news |last=Vance |first=Andrea |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/9326262/National-MPs-to-retire |title=National MPs to retire |date=25 October 2013 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=28 October 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Dr Paul Hutchison|url=http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/former/50PlibMpsFormerPaulHutchison1/hutchison-dr-paul|publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]]|access-date=7 July 2015}}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Port Waikato.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
In the 2019/2020 boundary review, the Representation Commission recreated it. This was necessitated by significant population growth in [[South Auckland]] and the [[Waikato]] region. It was created out of the western portion of {{NZ electorate link|Hunua}} and the northwestern area of {{NZ electorate link|Waikato}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020 |access-date= 18 April 2020}}</ref> Of the nine creations at that redistribution, it was one of the four included in the initial proposals.<ref>{{cite web |title=What's happening now in the boundary review |url= https://vote.nz/boundary-review-2019-2020/whats-happening-now-in-the-boundary-review/ |access-date=28 January 2020 | publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref>

In 2023, Neil Christensen, who was a candidate for the ACT Party, died during the advance voting period. This triggered the [[2023 Port Waikato by-election|Port Waikato by-election]] for the candidate vote, which will be held on 25 November 2023.<ref name="NZH 9 Oct">{{cite news |last1=Cheng |first1=Derek |title=Election 2023: Act candidate Neil Christensen dies, by-election to be held for Port Waikato |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/election-2023-act-candidate-neil-christensen-dies/N2Z4P5MVKBAY5DJ7PDWLW27Y24/ |access-date=9 October 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=9 October 2023}}</ref>

